---
import Layout from '../../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { sitePath } from '../../../lib/siteUrl';
---

<Layout title="Interface Overview — Varve Docs" description="Tour the Varve interface: toolbar, canvas, layers panel, inspector, timeline, status bar, and workspace modes.">
  <div class="container-custom">
    <div class="docs-page">
      <nav class="breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href={sitePath('/docs')}>Documentation</a> / <a href={sitePath('/docs/getting-started')}>Getting Started</a> / Interface Overview
      </nav>

      <h1>Interface Overview</h1>
      <p class="docs-intro">
        Varve's editor is organized into distinct zones that together form a powerful, efficient design workspace. Each panel serves a specific purpose and can be toggled or resized to suit your workflow.
      </p>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Shell Layout</h2>
        <p>
          The Varve editor uses a CSS Grid layout called the <strong>Shell</strong>. From top to bottom, left to right, the zones are:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Menubar</strong> — the top bar with File, Edit, View, Object, and other menus, alongside document renaming and <strong>dynamic context-aware shortcuts</strong> tailored to the current selection</li>
          <li><strong>Context bar</strong> — the thin strip directly under the menubar that follows the selection: nothing selected shows quick tool pills, an image shows Crop / Remove BG / Vectorize, a shape shows fill, stroke, and stroke width, a text layer shows font controls, a frame shows orientation and clip, and a multi-selection shows group, align, and boolean actions</li>
          <li><strong>Toolbar</strong> — the floating palette centred at the bottom of the canvas, holding the tools the active workspace declares (with flyout groups such as Shapes), a <strong>More tools</strong> control for anything that no longer fits, and <strong>Tool options</strong> for tools that have settings</li>
          <li><strong>Design Canvas navigator</strong> — the surface list above Layers for creating, naming, and switching open-ended design canvases</li>
          <li><strong>Design canvas</strong> — the central workspace where you create and edit your design, streamlined with hairline boundaries and direct-manipulation edge restore pills when panels are collapsed</li>
          <li><strong>Layers Panel</strong> — an elevated, collapsible floating container showing artwork on the active canvas (Ctrl+B) with a bespoke SVG collapse toggle and 14px splitter drag handle</li>
          <li><strong>Inspector Panel</strong> — an elevated, collapsible floating container (Ctrl+Shift+B) for editing the selection, active tool, and document properties</li>
          <li><strong>Status Bar</strong> — the bottom strip showing zoom level, canvas info, on-device AI status, and toggle controls</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section" aria-labelledby="surface-terms-heading">
        <h2 id="surface-terms-heading">Design canvases, frames, and publishing pages</h2>
        <p>
          Varve uses precise names for surfaces that behave differently. A
          <strong>design canvas</strong> is the open-ended editing view used for
          screens, exploration, components, and prototypes. A <strong>frame</strong>
          is an editable scene container inside that view; it has its own size,
          transform, clipping, auto layout, and direct export behavior.
        </p>
        <p>
          A <strong>publishing page</strong> is an ordered output surface for
          print and page-layout work. It carries trim, bleed, numbering,
          spreads, master pages, and page-aware export. The <strong>Pages
          panel</strong> manages those publishing pages, while the <strong>Page
          Navigator</strong> is the compact switcher along the bottom of the
          editor.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you are coming from Figma, its organizational “pages” are closest
          to Varve design canvases. Varve does not rename frames or turn them
          into publishing pages; switching workspaces changes which controls
          are disclosed while the same document and artwork remain intact.
        </p>
        <p>
          In the Design workspace, use <strong>Design canvases</strong> above
          the Layers panel to create, rename, duplicate, reorder, and switch
          surfaces. The active canvas itself is not a layer; only its frames,
          groups, text, images, and other authored objects appear in Layers.
          For trim, spreads, master pages, numbering, and print output, switch
          to the <strong>Print</strong> workspace and use <strong>Publishing
          pages</strong>.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Menubar</h2>
        <p>
          The menubar provides access to all of Varve's operations through familiar dropdown menus:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>File</strong> — New document, Open, Save (Ctrl+S), Save As (Ctrl+Shift+S), Import (Ctrl+I), Export (Ctrl+E)</li>
          <li><strong>Edit</strong> — Undo (Ctrl+Z), Redo (Ctrl+Shift+Z), Cut (Ctrl+X), Copy (Ctrl+C), Paste (Ctrl+V), Duplicate (Ctrl+D)</li>
          <li><strong>View</strong> — Zoom controls, toggle snap, toggle pixel grid, toggle panels, canvas mode switching, and the <strong>Workspace</strong> submenu (every mode, Reset, Customize, Manage Layouts)</li>
          <li><strong>Object</strong> — Group (Ctrl+G), Ungroup (Ctrl+Shift+G), Arrange (bring forward/send backward), Lock, boolean operations</li>
          <li><strong>Tools</strong> — Quick access to all drawing and editing tools</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Toolbar</h2>
        <p>
          The floating toolbar sits at the bottom centre of the canvas and contains the
          tools the active workspace declares. If the bottom edge is awkward on your
          setup — a laptop display edge or the macOS Dock can partially cover it —
          choose <strong>View &gt; Toolbar at Top</strong> to move it to the top of the
          canvas; the choice is remembered per workspace, and <strong>Toolbar at
          Bottom</strong> restores the default. Each tool has a keyboard shortcut for
          quick switching:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><code>V</code> — Select tool (move, resize, rotate)</li>
          <li><code>R</code> — Rectangle tool</li>
          <li><code>O</code> — Ellipse tool</li>
          <li><code>L</code> — Line tool</li>
          <li><code>A</code> — Arrow tool</li>
          <li><code>P</code> — Pen tool (bezier paths)</li>
          <li><code>Shift+P</code> — Pencil tool (freehand drawing)</li>
          <li><code>T</code> — Text tool</li>
          <li><code>H</code> — Hand tool (pan canvas)</li>
          <li><code>Z</code> — Zoom tool</li>
        </ul>
        <h3>Keyboard access</h3>
        <p>
          The toolbar is a single tab stop. Press <code>Tab</code> to enter it, then
          move between tools with the <strong>arrow keys</strong>; <code>Home</code> and
          <code>End</code> jump to the first and last tool. A tooltip shows each tool's
          name and current shortcut, and shortcuts you remap are reflected in both.
        </p>
        <h3>Groups, flyouts, and overflow</h3>
        <p>
          Related tools are grouped by separators, and a tool with a chevron opens a
          <strong>flyout</strong> menu — Shapes holds rectangle, ellipse, polygon, and
          star. When the window is too narrow for every tool, the palette keeps the
          tools you reach for most (creation first, then selection, editing, and
          navigation) and moves the rest behind the <strong>More tools</strong>
          control at the end of the row, which names how many tools it holds. The More
          menu groups the hidden tools by category, so nothing becomes unreachable.
        </p>
        <p>See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/keyboard-shortcuts')}>full keyboard shortcuts reference</a> and <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/vector')}>Vector Tools</a> for detailed usage.</p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Canvas</h2>
        <p>
          The canvas is your primary workspace. It supports standard design tool interactions:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Zoom</strong> — Mouse wheel to zoom in/out, anchored at cursor position. Ctrl+0 for 100%, Shift+1 to fit everything in view</li>
          <li><strong>Pan</strong> — Hold Space and drag, or middle-click drag to pan the canvas</li>
          <li><strong>View rotation</strong> — Available through View menu or status bar controls</li>
          <li><strong>Grid overlay</strong> — Toggle pixel grid and layout grid overlays from View menu or status bar</li>
          <li><strong>Snap</strong> — Scoped pointer assistance for visible edges, centers, guides, pages, frames, and configured grids. Toggle with <code>,</code> or the View menu; hold Ctrl/Cmd during a drag for a temporary bypass</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The canvas uses a <strong>floating origin</strong> system that maintains numerical precision even at extreme zoom levels or when working with very large documents.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Precision placement</h2>
        <p>
          Use the Inspector for exact coordinates, configured nudges, explicit alignment references,
          and distinct spacing modes. The canvas uses the same document geometry for its snap guides
          and applied movement. See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/precision-placement')}>Precision Placement guide</a> for details.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Layers Panel</h2>
        <p>
          The layers panel (right sidebar, top) displays your document as a hierarchical tree of nodes. Key features:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Tree view</strong> — Expandable/collapsible hierarchy showing parent-child relationships</li>
          <li><strong>Multi-select</strong> — Ctrl+click for disjoint selection, Shift+click for range selection</li>
          <li><strong>Drag and drop</strong> — Reorder and reparent nodes by dragging between tree entries</li>
          <li><strong>Filter/search</strong> — Type-ahead filtering to quickly find layers by name</li>
          <li><strong>Inline rename</strong> — Double-click or press F2 to rename any layer</li>
          <li><strong>Batch Rename</strong> — Right-click a layer and choose <em>Batch Rename…</em> to find and replace across the selection or every layer, with a live preview before anything changes</li>
          <li><strong>Context menu</strong> — Right-click for group, duplicate, delete, arrange, and copy/paste operations</li>
          <li><strong>Visibility and lock toggles</strong> — Eye icon to hide/show, lock icon to prevent editing</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Open <strong>Layer navigation settings</strong> in the Layers header to choose what an
          unmodified row activation does to the canvas: <strong>Select only</strong>,
          <strong>Reveal when needed</strong>, <strong>Center selection</strong>, or
          <strong>Fit selection</strong>. Focus-only keyboard movement and Shift/Ctrl multi-select
          keep the current camera. The separate <strong>Reveal canvas selection in Layers
          panel</strong> option controls the reverse direction and does not control canvas movement.
          Right-click a selection for one-shot <strong>Reveal on Canvas</strong>,
          <strong>Center Selection</strong>, or <strong>Zoom to Selection</strong> commands.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Inspector Panel</h2>
        <p>
          The inspector panel (right sidebar) is a contextual workspace for the current
          selection. The Design tab is the everyday Properties surface, organized around one
          canonical owner for each property group.
        </p>
        <p>
          With nothing selected, the Design tab follows the active tool. The <strong>Frame</strong>
          tool lists searchable size presets (devices first, with favorites and recents); picking
          one places a frame of that size and selects it. Tools with settings of their own, such as
          the brushes, marquees, magic wand, text, and crop, show a <strong>Show … options</strong>
          button that opens their Tool options beside the toolbar. Every other tool keeps the
          canvas and document settings in view, and the Page tool adds the page's print geometry at
          the top. A selected frame offers <strong>Resize to Preset</strong> directly below
          Position &amp; Size, including <strong>Save current size as preset</strong>.
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Position &amp; Size</strong> — X, Y, W, H, rotation, bounds, and aspect-ratio editing with mixed-selection support</li>
          <li><strong>Design</strong> — Stack / Grid, Appearance, fills, stroke, effects, and selection-aware content controls</li>
          <li><strong>Adjustments</strong> — Image and object treatment workflows when the selection supports them</li>
          <li><strong>Prototype</strong> — Interaction authoring for supported design and motion workflows</li>
          <li><strong>Export</strong> — Quick single-object export (format, scale, download/export), saved per-node export configurations, and generated code (SVG, CSS, Tailwind, CSS Modules, Flutter, SwiftUI)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          Tabs and sections are contextual. Each property is edited in one canonical section, so
          empty and mixed-selection states can communicate unavailable and mixed values directly.
          Specialized sections appear only when their owning node or workflow supports them:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Canvas &amp; Snapping</strong> — When nothing is selected, configure canvas background color, color mode, soft proofing, document/isometric grids, and global magnetic pointer snapping with customizable tolerance.</li>
          <li><strong>Mockups</strong> — For mockup frames, select templates, bind and replace artwork surfaces, tune placement (fit, alignment, rotation), and snapshot or flatten instances.</li>
          <li><strong>Masks</strong> — Add and tune vector masks, raster masks, and clipping masks with live density, feathering, inversion, and fill-rule controls.</li>
          <li><strong>Prototype Interactions</strong> — Configure triggers (click, hover, key press, delay), target screens, overlays, and smart animate transitions.</li>
          <li><strong>Section Customization</strong> — Open the Section Manager to hide, expand, or reorder inspector sections to suit your workflow, with preferences persisted per workspace.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          In Design, <strong>Position &amp; Size</strong> owns geometry and bounds while
          <strong>Stack / Grid</strong> owns the relationship between children (with collapsible Layout Guides for columns and rows). Fixed bounds remain
          visible but inactive; Relative fields show their authored percentage and reference. Fills,
          strokes, effects, typography, images, components, tables, and export each have one owner.
          Effect details open in a focused editor beside the rail while row actions stay available
          from an overflow menu.
          See the <a href={sitePath('/docs/tools/auto-layout')}>Auto Layout guide</a> for the sizing
          explanations and examples.
        </p>
        <h3>Align &amp; Distribute</h3>
        <p>
          The Align bar adapts to what the selection can actually do. The reference control always
          shows all three targets — <strong>Selection</strong>, <strong>Frame</strong>, and
          <strong>Page</strong> — with the active one highlighted; options that do not apply to the
          current selection stay visible but dimmed, and hovering explains why (for example,
          aligning to the selection bounds needs two or more layers). Every align button names its
          live target in its tooltip and accessible name ("Align left edges to parent frame"), so
          the commands never silently change meaning when the selection changes. With one layer
          selected the reference resolves to the nearest frame or the page; relative-only clusters
          — distribute, fixed gap, key object, tidy up, and oriented bounding boxes — appear once
          two or more layers are selected, and every one of those commands also stays reachable
          from the Object menu and its keyboard shortcut.
        </p>
        <h3>Position &amp; Size</h3>
        <p>
          W/H carry a proportion lock built from the standard linked/unlinked chain pair, so the
          locked state is unmistakable. The rotation row keeps R, flip H, flip V, and the skew
          disclosure on one line; skew fields auto-surface when a layer already carries shear and
          otherwise wait behind the diamond toggle.
        </p>
        <h3>Corner Radius</h3>
        <p>
          A single <strong>Radius</strong> field handles uniform rounding with an inline icon button
          to switch to per-corner mode. Independent corners render as a true <strong>2x2 grid</strong>
          — top-left and top-right above bottom-left and bottom-right, each beside its corner icon —
          so the fields map onto the shape instead of reading as a row of dials. Fields carry their
          px unit, and screen readers hear the full corner names. If a shape already has different
          radii per corner, the grid opens automatically. A <strong>Smoothing</strong> slider appears
          once any rounding exists, and corner radius works in multi-selection when any selected node
          is a rectangle or frame.
        </p>
        <h3>Image Placement &amp; Cropping</h3>
        <p>
          The five fit modes (Fill, Fit, Crop, Stretch, Tile) sit in one equal-width track. Scale and
          offset follow the fit mode: they are hidden for <strong>Stretch</strong>, which pins the
          image to the shape, with a one-line explanation, and reappear for every other mode.
          Selecting multiple images batches every change, and mixed fit or scale values surface as
          <em>Mixed</em> rather than silently adopting the first image. The Crop &amp; Bounds section
          keeps a six-up <strong>aspect ratio strip</strong> (Free, 1:1, 4:3, 16:9, 3:2, 9:16) plus
          collapsed Trim to Subject, Protect Faces, and Expand Bounds subsections.
        </p>
        <h3>Masks</h3>
        <p>
          When an eligible layer has no mask, one compact action group offers the applicable
          creations — Clip, Alpha, and Luminance for containers, Vector mask for leaves, and a Brush
          mask action — each with an icon and a tooltip that explains what the matte uses. With a
          mask active, the card presents a labelled <strong>Type</strong> row and
          <strong>Source</strong> picker, switches for <strong>Hide source</strong> and
          <strong>Link transform</strong>, Feather and Density fields, a segmented fill rule, and a
          vector-path edit trigger. Mask visibility and inversion are 24px icon toggles and removal
          is a danger-styled trash button; a collapsed Mask section still shows the active mask type
          beside its header so an enabled mask is never invisible.
        </p>
        <h3>Selection Colors</h3>
        <p>
          Selection Colors summarizes every colour in the selection — fills, strokes, gradient
          stops, table paints, and text ranges — with a swatch, monospace hex value, its role, a
          use count when a colour is shared, a copy-value button, and a
          <strong>select matching layers</strong> crosshair that targets every layer using that
          colour. To avoid duplicating the Fills section, it stays hidden when a single object's
          only colour is already editable one row below, and appears whenever a selection holds two
          or more colours or layers.
        </p>
        <h3>Typography</h3>
        <p>
          The Typography section keeps the everyday controls in one scannable spine: content,
          font family, weight, style, size, line height, letter spacing, and alignment. Alignment
          uses the standard icon set with named labels and tooltips instead of single letters, and
          case uses self-describing labels (None, ABC, abc, Abc). Tracking, paragraph spacing,
          vertical alignment, direction, writing mode, case, decoration, list style, overflow, and
          text sizing live in a collapsed <strong>Advanced typography</strong> subsection whose
          header badge reports how many of those properties the current layer already uses.
          Vertical alignment appears only for text with a container (area text), and vertical
          orientation appears only when the writing mode is vertical. OpenType features list only
          the tags the selected face reports — required shaping features are never offered as
          disabled rows — use the inspector's shared labelled dropdowns, and show a count of
          active features while collapsed. Glyph adjustments are a collapsed subsection with a
          count badge, and the whole section remains hideable from the Section Manager.
        </p>
        <h3>Insights</h3>
        <p>
          Insights is the document-level review surface, collapsed by default at the end of the
          Design tab and hideable from the Section Manager like any optional section. Tabs use
          plain names — Review, Contrast, Spacing, Names, Governance, Design debt, Prototype,
          Auto layout, Components, Similar layers, and Linter — and are filtered by
          applicability: Spacing needs two or more layers, Names needs a selection, Auto layout
          needs a frame, and Prototype needs prototype data, so a tab never opens onto a dead
          end. Tabs a workspace treats as secondary (Spacing and Names in Design) stay reachable
          from the More menu. Similar layers stays available with nothing selected because its
          empty state is a working natural-language search field. The Review tab filters findings
          by category and severity, reports how many findings were suppressed this session with a
          Restore action, and only shows actions that actually change the document.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Status Bar</h2>
        <p>
          The status bar at the bottom of the editor provides at-a-glance information and quick toggles:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li>Zoom percentage with unit selector (px, pt, mm, cm, in, pc)</li>
          <li>Pixel grid toggle</li>
          <li>Snap toggle</li>
          <li>Fit selection to viewport button</li>
          <li>Canvas mode indicator (Full / Outline / Preview)</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Canvas Modes</h2>
        <p>
          Varve supports three canvas display modes, accessible from the View menu or status bar:
        </p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Full</strong> — All fills, effects, and overlays rendered (default)</li>
          <li><strong>Outline</strong> — Fills and effects stripped, uniform stroke-only display for focusing on structure</li>
          <li><strong>Preview</strong> — All overlays hidden for a clean preview of the final design</li>
        </ul>
      </section>

      <section class="docs-section">
        <h2>Workspace Modes</h2>
        <p>
          The menubar carries the eight task-focused <a href={sitePath('/docs/workspaces')}>workspaces</a>
          as a switcher: Design, Print, Draw, Photo, Motion, Logo, Email, and Codegen. The active
          workspace is always named in its accent pill; the others are icons, and any mode that no
          longer fits moves into the switcher's overflow menu rather than disappearing. Each
          workspace curates the toolbar and workflow for its task, and every mode edits the same
          document with the same selection, viewport, and undo history — switch with
          <code>Ctrl+Shift+1</code> through <code>Ctrl+Shift+7</code>, and
          <code>Ctrl+Shift+9</code> for Codegen.
        </p>
        <p>
          The toolbar itself is composed per workspace: tools, their order, and flyout
          groups all come from the workspace's configuration, so the same horizontal
          palette leads with the tools each task actually needs. See the
          <a href={sitePath('/docs/workspaces')}>Workspaces guide</a> for the full mode table.
        </p>
      </section>

      <div class="docs-cta">
        <h2>Next Steps</h2>
        <p>Continue with the <a href={sitePath('/docs/getting-started/first-project')}>Your First Project</a> tutorial to put this knowledge into practice.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</Layout>

